# 022 · Launch a rig on RunPod

**Status:** `IN_PROGRESS` · **Version:** [v0.1.0](../roadmap.md#v010) · **Part:** Rigs on RunPod · **Depends on:** [010](010-published-images.goal), [019](019-rig-only-entry.goal), [020](020-runpod-rig-image.goal), [021](021-enrollment-codes.goal)

## What

A launch form: the GPU type and count (from RunPod's list, with prices), the cloud type, the data center, the storage and the image. The hourly price updates as you change them, and choices that cannot apply are hidden. With it, the rig's page shows the cost per hour and the spend so far. A spend limit and an idle stop apply only when the owner sets them. Launch also works over REST, MCP and the CLI.

## Why

Rigs on RunPod GPUs are the owner's priority feature: quantization, kernel and engine work on GPUs the owner does not own, paid by the hour.

## Done when

- [x] A mockup was approved before the build. (The owner approved `v010-runpod-launch.html` and `v010-runpod-rig.html` on 2026-10-20, with the idle stop's change: see Decisions.)
- [ ] The owner launches a rig from the dashboard. It enrolls and appears under Rigs with its price. (Built: **Launch on RunPod** at `/ui/rigs/add/runpod`, reached from **Add a rig** and the account's row in **Providers**, launches with the existing backend; the rig's page follows it to its first report and shows its price; `src/control/runpod-ui.test.ts` against the fake RunPod API. Left: the owner's first launch on RunPod, and the price on the **Rigs** list, with the list's revised mockup.)
- [x] Spend is shown, and a set limit and a set idle stop both work. (The rig's page shows the cost now, the spend so far with a bar to the limit, the spend limit and the idle stop, and changes them; a limit and the idle stop stop the pod, its results first: `src/control/runpod-ui.test.ts`, `src/control/runpod-rigs.test.ts`; pages: Run a rig on RunPod, A rig's page, REST API › When a rig is idle.)

## Decisions

- 2026-10-17, owner: The launch form hides choices that cannot apply, and the price follows every change. A RunPod rig's page is a normal rig page, with an event log.
- 2026-10-15, owner: No default caps, so spend controls are fields that the owner fills in.
- 2026-10-20, owner: Only the owner may launch, as recommended. The operator token that agents use may not.
- 2026-10-20, owner: Secure cloud on demand first, as recommended. Community and spot come later.
- 2026-10-20, lead: following the owner's decision above, a launch is on Secure cloud on demand only. Control refuses Community (the field stays, for a later version), and spot is not offered.
- 2026-10-20, lead: The form and the rig page's cost wait for the owner's mockup approval. Everything behind them is built first: REST, MCP, the command line, the price and the spend, and the limits.
- 2026-10-20, owner: The launch form: approved, with one change: when no campaign is running on a RunPod rig, it stops after a timeout so it costs no GPU time, and starting a run wakes it again.
- 2026-10-20, builder: The idle stop counts the time no run is at work: a running run is at work while a lane's trial is in progress or a judge job is queued for it; a judge job that runs counts whatever its run; between trials, a running run's newest event is its last work. A run that is paused, finished, or running with nothing under way (blocked) is idle, and the stop pauses it; the next start resumes it.
- 2026-10-20, builder: Assigning or resuming a run on a stopped RunPod pod starts the pod first (REST, MCP, the CLI and the dashboard's Start a run, new campaign and Resume). The command is checked and signed before anything starts; a refused start refuses the command, and nothing is queued. Only the owner's token starts a pod; MCP refuses an operator's or agent's command for a stopped pod.
- 2026-10-20, builder: The idle stop's default lives in `DEFAULT_IDLE_STOP_MINUTES` (none) until the owner confirms on-at-30-minutes or none. `null` at launch means none explicitly; the form pre-fills the default and sends none when the owner clears it.
- 2026-10-20, builder: The launch form offers what the backend launches: Secure cloud on demand, the GPU types on Secure cloud that have stock (cheapest first), 1 to 8 GPUs with the judge's share in the rig's settings (`THERMITE_JUDGE_GPUS`), a data center or any, a pod volume (network volumes are not offered: the launch has none), control's image or one of the owner's, the limits and the name. The price is the GPU's list price on Secure cloud times the GPUs, as the backend counts it before RunPod gives one; the form says that RunPod also bills the disk and the volume, which RunPod's list does not price. Its "What happens" follows the enrollment design: the pod's settings hold the hash of control's signing key, not the key. The mockup's read-only sign-in does not exist: the dashboard signs in with the owner's token alone.

## Open questions

None.

## Progress

- 2026-10-19: Goal written from the v0.1.0 release plan.
- 2026-10-20: Built behind the screen (`src/control/runpod-rigs.ts`, `runpod-rig-view.ts`):
  - **Launch, the owner's alone:** `POST /v1/gpu-clouds/<name>/rigs`, the MCP tool `runpod_launch` (registered for the owner's token only) and `thermite runpod launch`. Control's RunPod key from Providers › GPU clouds, through `runpod.ts`'s client. A fresh read of RunPod's list checks the GPU type on the chosen cloud and the data center; then `ControlTunnels.mint` (the rig's settings: `cuda-gpu` on all the pod's GPUs unless the owner gives others), `createPod` (the one-container image from `THERMITE_RUNPOD_RIG_IMAGE` or the request, the GPU type and count, Secure by default, the data center, a 40 GB container disk and a 100 GB volume at `/workspace` by default, port 8790 as HTTP, only the launch values in its environment, the image's own entry point) and `attach`. A launch RunPod refuses drops the minted rig (`dropTunnelRig`), so its code can never enroll; an answer control can't use removes the pod.
  - **Price and spend:** control reads each pod every 30 s (`advance`, in control's loop): its status, RunPod's price for it (the list price times the GPUs until RunPod gives one) and the spend so far, counted while the pod runs. In `GET /v1/rigs` (`runpod`), `GET /v1/rigs/<rig>/runpod`, `GET /v1/gpu-clouds/<name>/rigs`, the rig's view (`runpod`), the MCP tools `runpod_rigs` and `list_rigs`, and `thermite runpod list`.
  - **Limits, only when the owner sets them:** a spend limit and an idle stop, at launch or later (`POST /v1/rigs/<rig>/runpod/limits`, `runpod_rig_limits`, `thermite runpod limits`; `null` or `off` clears one). A pod that reaches one is stopped (its volume stays) and the audit log says why (`runpod_rig_stopped`). Idle time counts from enrollment, the pod's last start or the setting of the stop, whichever is latest, and only while no run runs on the rig, from its signed reports.
  - Tests (`src/control/runpod-rigs.test.ts`, `src/cli/runpod.test.ts`) against a fake RunPod API that refuses any other host: the pod's body and environment, GPUs and a data center, Community refused, every refusal starting nothing and leaving no rig, a refused pod leaving no rig, price and spend over 100 hours with nothing stopped, the spend limit stopping once at the limit, the idle stop only after enrollment and only with no run running, limits set and cleared later, REST and MCP refusing every token but the owner's.
  - Pages: REST API (Rigs on a GPU cloud, the audit actions), MCP tools, Command line, Settings (`THERMITE_RUNPOD_RIG_IMAGE`), Tokens and roles, the image reference, Trust between control and rigs.
  - 2026-10-20 (goal 023): a limit now stops the pod through the lifecycle's stop: the runs paused and the results sent out first, then RunPod's stop; never a removal. A start through control is refused while the spend limit is reached. The launch is the first step of the rig's history (`runpod_launched`).
  - 2026-10-20: The image. Each release publishes the one-container rig image, and its `compose.yaml` makes it control's `THERMITE_RUNPOD_RIG_IMAGE`, pinned by digest (goals 010 and 011): a tester's launch needs no image. `compose.yaml` passes the setting from `.env` too. [Run a rig on RunPod](../../_site/src/content/docs/how-to/install/runpod-rig.md) launches with control's image by default; building and pushing one's own is its section "Use a rig image of your own". The first published rig image waits for the first tag.
  - Left then: the mockup and the screens (the launch form, and the price and spend on the rig's page), and the owner's first paid launch, which also confirms the data center and volume fields of RunPod's create-a-pod body (the client sends `dataCenterId` and `volume: {size, mountPath}`; the spike checked only the fields test pods send). Getting a Hugging Face token to the supervisor without RunPod's settings (goal 024) is not built: the exchange already carries sealed answers to the supervisor's calls (model routes with their keys), so a route control answers with a sealed token is one way.
- 2026-10-20: Idle stop meaning and waking built (`d1435d3`). Tests: `runpod-rigs.test.ts`, `runpod-lifecycle.test.ts` (waking over REST, MCP and the dashboard), `src/cli/assign.test.ts`, `src/cli/runpod.test.ts`. Pages: REST API (Signed commands, When a rig is idle), MCP tools, Command line, Signed commands, Limits and pacing.
- 2026-10-20: The screens, from the approved mockups. **Launch on RunPod** (`src/control/ui/runpod-launch.ts`, its script `runpod-launch-client.ts`): RunPod's GPUs with prices and stock, hidden choices, the price following each choice, every label with its tip, **Read again**, refusals that keep what was typed. A RunPod rig's page: **The pod** with the cost now, the spend so far, the spend limit and the idle stop, changed in place (`/ui/rigs/<rig>/runpod/limits`); before its first report, **Getting it ready**. Tests: `src/control/runpod-ui.test.ts`. Pages: Run a rig on RunPod (now from the dashboard), A rig's page, the image reference and How it fits together. Left: the owner's first launch on RunPod, and the price on the **Rigs** list with its revised mockup.

